#
# pre-push — local quality gate before pushing (see CONTRIBUTING.md).
# Installed by `task setup` / `task hooks` (git config core.hooksPath .githooks).
#
# Runs the GREEN subset of CI: publication, structural and documentation-honesty
# lints; console typechecking; a full Go build; and the race-enabled test suite.
# golangci-lint (lint:go / lint:web) is intentionally NOT run here: golangci-lint
# v2 cannot typecheck under Go 1.26 yet (repo-wide), so it exits non-zero with 0
# issues and would block every push. The full toolchain in CI covers it.
#
# BRANCH-AWARE SINCE 2026-08-02 (GATES v3). What runs depends on WHERE the commits
# land, not on who is pushing: main and tags take the full gate, feature refs take
# the fast lints only. The rule itself lives in scripts/prepush-refclass.sh so it can
# be tested without paying for the gate it decides (scripts/test-prepush-refclass.sh,
# `task lint:prepush-refclass`). Until today the policy said one thing and this file
# did another — every lane ran the full gate on every branch and worked around it by
# hand with OLIVARES_FAST_PUSH=1, which also silently opened the same hole on main.
#
# SANCTIONED bypass: git push --no-verify (declare it in the PR). It is not the only
# technically possible one — a client hook can always be sidestepped (core.hooksPath, or
# simply editing this file), which is exactly why the boundary that actually enforces
# anything is mainline-ci plus branch protection on the server, not this script.
#
# ============ LIMITATIONS, DECLARED — of THIS piece (2026-08-03) ======================
# The split landed on its own, deliberately narrowed to what an eleven-round adversarial
# review verified (an internal design note (not shipped)). These are the
# things it does NOT promise. Each is something a reader could otherwise infer from the
# guarantees above, and none of them is closed by the code below.
#
#  N-1  THE CLASSIFIER RUNS LIKE ANY OTHER COMMAND THIS HOOK CALLS: in the foreground, with
#       no deadline of its own. A rule that never returns parks the lane exactly as a wedged
#       `task test` would. It is a local, hermetic script with no network and no lock. The
#       base hook already had unbounded commands on content pushes; classification newly
#       places the same failure mode BEFORE the deletion/skip decisions, which the base hook
#       answered from its own shell loop without running anything (round 12, M-03).
#  N-2  THE VERDICT IS JUDGED WHEN THE CLASSIFIER EXITS. Its stdout goes to a file and is
#       read back as BYTES — a NUL anywhere in it refuses, which a bash variable could not
#       even represent — but the read happens once, when the process this hook started is
#       done. A descendant the classifier leaves behind holding the same stdout can still
#       write after that snapshot, and those bytes are not judged. The classifier in this
#       tree forks nothing; the limit is on the contract, not on today's implementation.
#  N-3  THIS HOOK GATES WHAT IT CALLS, NOT WHAT ITS TOOLS RUN. The split's guarantee ("a
#       feature ref runs no heavy task") is proved by scripts/test-prepush-refclass.sh over
#       the calls THIS FILE makes. What go-task expands from Taskfile.yml behind one of
#       those calls, and what a task's own script reaches for (`bash scripts/foo.sh` running
#       `go test` with no Taskfile edge), are outside that observation and outside the claim.
#       See DEFERRED D-2: this is the open half of the property, not a satisfied one.
#  N-4  THE HOST MUTEX IS THE ONE THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY HAD, UNCHANGED. The split moved
#       WHO takes it: every push classified `full` — main, tags, and any deny-closed
#       promotion (unknown namespace, malformed line) — instead of every lane on the box.
#       Nothing else moved. Its known weaknesses are therefore exactly today's: the lock's age is
#       the commit date, which `git commit-tree` takes from GIT_COMMITTER_DATE and a slow
#       host clock changes on its own, so a LIVE holder can be presumed dead and robbed;
#       the takeover is a plain force push, so two lanes that saw the same stale OID can
#       both believe they won; and release deletes the ref unconditionally and swallows
#       every error, so a lane that was stolen from can later delete its successor's lock.
#       Measured in rounds 3 and 5 of the review. See DEFERRED D-1.
#  N-5  A CLIENT HOOK IS NOT A SECURITY BOUNDARY. `--no-verify`, `core.hooksPath` and an
#       editor all defeat it, and the classification is only as good as the stdin git hands
#       it. What enforces anything about `main` is the server: mainline-ci plus branch
#       protection.
#
# ============ DEFERRED WORK — NOT accepted limits, unresolved defects =================
# These were built, reviewed and TAKEN BACK OUT of this change because they did not pass
# the contrast. They are named here so nobody reads N-3/N-4 as a decision to live with
# them. Finding IDs are from an internal design note (not shipped), round 11.
#
#  D-1  MUTEX HARDENING — owner-safe release and takeover under --force-with-lease on the
#       exact OID, liveness from the holder's PROCESS (host + PID namespace + boot id +
#       /proc start-time) instead of a date the holder wrote, a named door for breaking a
#       confirmed-live holder, per-call network deadlines and a wait budget that is an
#       actual deadline. Round 11 left L-02 OPEN: with the shipped defaults
#       (WAIT == STALE) the promised recovery of an indeterminable holder is UNREACHABLE —
#       the lane gives up at the threshold before the lease is ever re-evaluated, so an
#       abandoned lock on another host is never recovered automatically. Landing a mutex
#       whose advertised recovery cannot fire is worse than the plain one above, which at
#       least does what its comment says. REOPEN with the ordering of lease vs give-up
#       fixed, or with WAIT > STALE pinned and stated.
#       ✅ CERRADO 2026-08-16 por la segunda via: STALE es ahora GATE_LOCK_STALE_SECS y WAIT se
#       deriva como STALE+1800, con una guarda que lo corrige y lo DICE si alguien lo fija por
#       debajo. El robo se evalua siempre antes de rendirse. Probado en scripts/test-gate-lock-order.sh.
#  D-2  TASKFILE-GRAPH OBSERVATION — resolving what go-task expands from Taskfile.yml so a
#       feature lane cannot buy a heavy task through a `deps:` edge the hook never types
#       (N-3). Round 11 left L-01 OPEN: the hand-written resolver is blind to syntax go-task
#       3.51.1 accepts and executes — single-quoted entries and flow mappings
#       (`deps: ['heavy']`, `deps: [{task: heavy}]`) produced ZERO edges while `task --dry`
#       ran the task. A closure that silently misses valid forms certifies the property it
#       cannot see, which is worse than not claiming it. REOPEN using go-task's own graph as
#       the authority, not another partial YAML parser.
#       ✅ CERRADO 2026-08-16 (a repository gate) por esa vía exacta: `scripts/check-taskfile-graph.sh`
#       ⚠ MATIZADO 2026-08-18: la observación era correcta y el CONJUNTO no. El ancla del
#       carril rápido exigía `task` en columna 0, y este fichero llama a
#       `lint:commit-identity` INDENTADO dentro de un condicional: 80 tareas observadas
#       de 81. Verificado por mutación —`deps: [build:cloud]` en esa tarea daba OK— y
#       corregido con una extracción que pela sangrado y prefijos de entorno, de forma
#       que `GOFLAGS=… task test` tampoco pueda caerse de la lista PESADA.
#       pregunta a `task --dry`, que es go-task resolviendo SU grafo, y compara el conjunto
#       alcanzado con el gate pesado. Las DOS listas —rápidas y pesadas— se derivan de ESTE
#       fichero cortando por `task lint:prepush-refclass`, el mismo corte que usa la batería, así
#       que no pueden discrepar sobre dónde está la línea ni caducar cuando alguien añade un lint.
#       Verificado por mutación con las dos formas a las que el parser a mano era ciego:
#       `deps: ['test']` y `deps: [{task: build:go}]` — las caza las dos y nombra el cruce.
#       Coste medido: 3 s para 63 tareas rápidas contra 11 pesadas. Cableado como
#       `task lint:taskfile-graph` en el carril rápido, con la lista declarada actualizada en el
#       mismo commit (268 passed, 0 failed).
#  D-3  DETERMINISM OF THE MUTEX FIXTURES — round 11 measured the same bytes giving
#       `265 passed, 1 failed` and `266 passed, 0 failed` on two consecutive runs (L-03):
#       a wait-budget mutant that raced whole-second clock arithmetic. `lint:prepush-refclass`
#       is in the fast lints of EVERY push with commits, and an intermittent gate is worse
#       than a slow one — it teaches lanes to re-run until green. Nothing time-dependent
#       came back with this change; anything that does must force its overshoot
#       deterministically instead of measuring the wall clock.
# =====================================================================================
set -euo pipefail

# ── INSTANTÁNEA: este hook NO se relee del árbol de trabajo mientras corre ───────────
# bash lee un script POR DESPLAZAMIENTO DE BYTES a la vez que lo ejecuta. Si alguien
# edita este fichero con un push EN VUELO, bash sigue leyendo desde el offset que tenía
# guardado, que ahora apunta a otro contenido, y ejecuta el trozo que caiga ahí.
#
# Medido el 2026-08-18, y el coste no fue el push: una edición de SEIS líneas en esta
# cabecera, 14 s antes, mató un push a `main` con
#     .githooks/pre-push: line 543: the: command not found
# — prosa de un comentario ejecutada como comando. Y el mensaje MIENTE dos veces: la
# línea 543 de este fichero es `task lint:verifier-truth`, que corre verde en 1 s, y el
# número invita a buscar un defecto donde no hay ninguno. Con cinco carriles que editan
# este hook y empujan a la vez, no es una rareza: es el caso normal de una jornada.
#
# El remedio es correr desde una copia y BORRARLA acto seguido. El descriptor sigue
# abierto, el inodo vive hasta que el proceso termina, y el contenido deja de tener
# nombre en el sistema de ficheros: no hay nada que editar. No hace falta limpiarla —
# lo que evita chocar con el `trap release_gate_lock EXIT` de más abajo, que sería el
# único sitio donde poner una limpieza y que la borraría de un plumazo.
if [ -z "${OLIVARES_HOOK_SNAPSHOT:-}" ]; then
	# Si TMPDIR apunta a un directorio que no existe —pasa, y está medido— mktemp falla;
	# se reintenta en /tmp antes de rendirse. El noexec de /tmp da igual: esto se invoca
	# como `bash <fichero>`, no se ejecuta el fichero.
	_snap="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/olivares-prepush.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null || mktemp "/tmp/olivares-prepush.XXXXXX")" || {
		echo "pre-push: ⛔ NO HE PODIDO MIRAR: no puedo crear la instantánea del hook." >&2
		exit 2
	}
	if ! cat -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" >"$_snap"; then
		rm -f -- "$_snap"
		echo "pre-push: ⛔ NO HE PODIDO MIRAR: no puedo leer el hook para la instantánea." >&2
		exit 2
	fi
	OLIVARES_HOOK_SNAPSHOT="$_snap" exec bash "$_snap" "$@"
fi
rm -f -- "$OLIVARES_HOOK_SNAPSHOT"


# The ambient git environment OUTRANKS `-C`: with GIT_DIR exported — which git does
# from every LINKED worktree, i.e. from every parallel session — this script's throwaway
# repositories would be driven into the LIVE repository instead. Measured 2026-08-06;
# it left the branch of PR #526 pointing at a fixture commit. Fail closed: a missing
# sanitiser is "I could not isolate", never "isolation was not needed".
# Resolved from the WORKING DIRECTORY, not from $0, and for the same reason every other
# path in this hook is: git runs a hook with its cwd at the top of the worktree, which is
# why `bash scripts/prepush-refclass.sh` below works. Resolving from $0 instead looked
# equivalent and was not — the battery copies this file to the ROOT of a fixture rather
# than into a `.githooks/` subdirectory, so `$0/../scripts` pointed one level above the
# fixture and every e2e case died at exit 2. One idiom for paths, not two.
_olivares_git_env="scripts/lib/git-env.sh"
# shellcheck source=/dev/null
. "$_olivares_git_env" || {
	echo "pre-push: cannot source $_olivares_git_env — the throwaway repositories the" >&2
	echo "pre-push: gate builds could be driven into THIS repository. Refusing." >&2
	exit 2
}
unset _olivares_git_env

# --- WHICH GATE DOES THIS PUSH DESERVE? ---------------------------------------------
# Git feeds one "<local ref> <local oid> <remote ref> <remote oid>" line per ref on
# stdin. The remote ref is the load-bearing field and this hook used to discard it.
#
# The classifier reads THE HOOK'S OWN stdin, directly. The first revision did
# `push_refs="$(cat)"` and fed it back through a here-string: that round trip strips
# trailing newlines and appends one of its own, so the bytes the rule judged were not the
# bytes git sent. A rule cannot be deny-closed about input it never saw. Nothing
# downstream reads stdin again — the classifier consumes it to EOF.
if [ ! -r scripts/prepush-refclass.sh ]; then
	echo "pre-push: scripts/prepush-refclass.sh is missing — this hook cannot tell which gate" >&2
	echo "pre-push: the push needs, so it refuses. Not knowing is not the same as being clean." >&2
	exit 1
fi
# CAPTURE BYTES, NOT A BASH STRING. `$(...)` strips EVERY trailing LF — a classifier
# printing `fast<TAB>reason<LF><LF>` satisfied a contract that says "exactly one line" —
# and it also DELETES NUL bytes, warning on a stderr nobody reads inside a hook. Measured:
# `fa<NUL>st<TAB>reason<LF>` arrived as `fast` and bought a MAIN push the fast lane, and
# `de<NUL>lete` arrived as `delete` and took the no-toolchain exception below. A bash
# variable cannot even hold that byte, so the verdict does not travel through one: stdout
# goes to a FILE and is read back byte-exactly. `read -r -d ''` stops at a NUL, so the read
# SUCCEEDING is the proof one was there; at EOF it fails with every byte read, trailing LFs
# included. The redirection is not a pipeline, so `$?` is the classifier's OWN status —
# "the reader worked" is not "the rule agreed" (N-2 states what this does not cover).
gate_out=""
if ! gate_out="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/olivares-prepush-verdict.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null)" ||
	[ -z "$gate_out" ]; then
	echo "pre-push: could not create a temp file to capture the classification — refusing the" >&2
	echo "pre-push: push. A verdict this hook could not even receive has cleared nothing." >&2
	exit 1
fi
# STDIN IS CAPTURED TO A FILE FIRST, and both properties the comment above defends survive it.
# Byte-exactness: the objection was to `$(...)`, which strips trailing LFs and deletes NULs
# because a bash VARIABLE cannot hold them. A file can, and `cat` copies bytes. Not a pipeline:
# the classifier still gets a plain `<` redirection, so `$?` is still its OWN status.
#
# It is captured because a fast lint may need to know WHICH refs are travelling. The lint that
# first required it, lint:unpublished-work, was withdrawn from this lane the same day (see below),
# and the capture stays because it is the correct shape regardless: a hook that consumed its own
# stdin and could not say what it was pushing had to re-derive it, and re-deriving is where the
# earlier byte-exactness bugs came from.
#
# A capture that FAILS refuses the push: a classifier fed a truncated file would see fewer refs
# than git sent, and fewer refs is the direction that buys a main push the fast lane.
push_refs_file=""
if ! push_refs_file="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/olivares-prepush-refs.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null)" ||
	[ -z "$push_refs_file" ]; then
	rm -f "$gate_out"
	echo "pre-push: could not create a temp file for the pushed refs — refusing the push." >&2
	exit 1
fi
if ! cat >"$push_refs_file"; then
	rm -f "$gate_out" "$push_refs_file"
	echo "pre-push: could not read the pushed refs from stdin — refusing. A hook that did not" >&2
	echo "pre-push: receive the refs has classified nothing." >&2
	exit 1
fi
# Y SE RECOGE AL SALIR, por CUALQUIER camino. Hasta el 2026-08-19 se borraba en una sola
# rama —la de stdin ilegible, linea de arriba— y en la del EXITO, que es la normal, no se
# borraba nunca: /tmp acumulaba 47.520 ficheros `olivares-prepush-refs.*`, uno por push, en
# un tmpfs de 2G que es RAM. No puede borrarse antes de terminar porque se EXPORTA y lo leen
# check-commit-identity y check-web-bundle-freshness, que sin el responden «NO HE PODIDO
# MIRAR» — es decir, un borrado temprano convertiria dos gates en ciegos. Por eso va en un
# trap, y por eso el trap de mas abajo (release_gate_lock) tiene que hacer las DOS cosas: un
# segundo `trap ... EXIT` SUSTITUYE al primero, no se suma.
trap 'rm -f "${push_refs_file:-}"' EXIT

# ⛔ Y EL REMOTO AL QUE SE EMPUJA, que hasta hoy no viajaba. El gate de identidad excluye contra
# `origin/main` para no cobrar por lo ya publicado; si el push va a OTRO remoto, esos commits no
# están publicados allí y la exclusión mide contra el sitio equivocado, callando. Hoy es inerte —
# este clon tiene un solo remoto, medido — pero es latente: basta añadir un segundo. Git nos da el
# nombre en $1 y la URL en $2; no pasarlos era tirar el dato que resuelve la pregunta.
export OLIVARES_PUSH_REFS_FILE="$push_refs_file"
export OLIVARES_PUSH_REMOTE_NAME="${1:-}"
# Y LA URL, que es la que de verdad engaña: `remote.origin.pushurl` puede apuntar a otro sitio con
# el MISMO nombre, así que comparar sólo el nombre deja pasar exactamente el caso que importa.
export OLIVARES_PUSH_REMOTE_URL="${2:-}"
gate_rule_rc=0
bash scripts/prepush-refclass.sh <"$push_refs_file" >"$gate_out" || gate_rule_rc=$?
if [ "$gate_rule_rc" != "0" ]; then
	rm -f "$gate_out"
	echo "pre-push: ref classification FAILED — refusing the push (a gate that could not look" >&2
	echo "pre-push: at the refs has not cleared them)." >&2
	exit 1
fi
gate_decision=""
gate_nul=0
if IFS= read -r -d '' gate_decision <"$gate_out"; then gate_nul=1; fi
rm -f "$gate_out"
if [ "$gate_nul" = "1" ]; then
	echo "pre-push: the classification carries a NUL byte — refusing the push. The verdict is a" >&2
	echo "pre-push: contract in BYTES, and a stream this shell cannot even represent is not one" >&2
	echo "pre-push: line of it." >&2
	exit 1
fi
gate_capture="$gate_decision"

# THE SHAPE OF THE VERDICT IS PART OF THE CONTRACT. Reading the first word of the first
# line accepted a classifier that printed `fast` and then, on the next line, `full`: the
# hook believed the first, ran the fast lane on a main push and exited 0. A tool that
# contradicts itself has classified nothing, and "it said fast somewhere" is not a
# verdict. Exactly ONE line — one terminating LF and no other — exactly
# `<full|fast|skip|delete><TAB><non-empty reason>`, no CR, no second TAB. Anything else is
# the third answer — I COULD NOT LOOK — and refuses.
gate_tab=$'\t'
gate_usable=1
case "$gate_decision" in
*$'\n')
	# The ONE LF the contract allows is the one that terminates the line. Whatever is left
	# after removing it must contain none.
	gate_decision="${gate_decision%$'\n'}"
	;;
*) gate_usable=0 ;; # no terminating LF at all: not a line
esac
case "$gate_decision" in
*$'\n'* | *$'\r'*) gate_usable=0 ;;
esac
if [ "$gate_usable" = "1" ] &&
	[[ ! $gate_decision =~ ^(full|fast|skip|delete)${gate_tab}[^${gate_tab}]+$ ]]; then
	gate_usable=0
fi
if [ "$gate_usable" != "1" ]; then
	printf 'pre-push: unusable classification %q — refusing the push (one line,\n' \
		"$gate_capture" >&2
	echo "pre-push: '<full|fast|skip|delete><TAB><reason>', is the whole contract)." >&2
	exit 1
fi
gate_class="${gate_decision%%"${gate_tab}"*}"
gate_reason="${gate_decision#*"${gate_tab}"}"

# THE CLASS TRAVELS TO THE LINTS THAT NEED IT, exported HERE -- where it is resolved and long
# before any lint runs -- because this hook is the only actor that knows the answer.
# `check-hub-web-fidelity.sh` has to tell a feature-branch push (where drift against the published
# web is a NOTICE with an owner, because whoever pushes cannot fix the public site) from a push to
# `main` or a tag (where you are PUBLISHING, and shipping against a desynchronised storefront is
# the exact harm that gate exists to prevent: 2026-08-14, the OpenAPI an evaluator reads 151 lines
# behind the real one).
#
# IT IS NOT RE-DERIVED DOWNSTREAM. A second copy of this rule would drift from
# `prepush-refclass.sh` the day someone adds a namespace -- the trap
# `web-bundle-source-digest.sh` documents with its measurement. The gate RECEIVES the verdict.
#
# AND ITS ABSENCE IS STRICT, not permissive: a script invoked by hand, or by a harness that does
# not export it, gets the blocking behaviour and says so. Same rule this hook already applies to a
# namespace it does not recognise -- not knowing what something is does not authorise running less.
export OLIVARES_PUSH_CLASS="$gate_class"

# THE ONE NAMED EXCEPTION: A PURE DELETION PUSH.
# The `task` check below was moved in FRONT of the skip verdict so the written invariant
# ("no task -> refusal, never green") would be true with no footnote. Measured with real
# git on a machine without go-task, that cost this: `git push --delete stale-branch` was
# REFUSED and the only way to tidy a branch was to disable the entire hook with
# --no-verify — i.e. the absolute rule made lanes practise the bypass on the one operation
# that needs no gate at all.
#
# So the exception is NAMED rather than left implicit: a push in which EVERY ref line is a
# deletion carries no commits, so there is nothing any lane could lint, build or test, and
# it does not need the toolchain to prove that. The classifier decides this — verdict
# `delete`, which requires deletion_refs == lines_seen — so the hook is not re-deriving it
# from a reason string, and a deletion travelling with a gate-lock ref, a commit or an
# unreadable line is NOT this class. Everything else keeps the unconditional check below,
# `skip` included. Documented in CLAUDE.md and CONTRIBUTING.md under this same name.
if [ "$gate_class" = "delete" ]; then
	echo "pre-push: ${gate_reason} — the named deletion exception: no gate, and no toolchain" >&2
	echo "pre-push: is required to run none. Every other class still refuses without 'task'." >&2
	exit 0
fi

# DENY-CLOSED, AND UNCONDITIONALLY SO. A gate that silently skips when its tool is
# missing is not a gate — this very machine shipped without `task` on PATH and the hook
# printed a skip and passed. The binary usually exists at ~/go/bin/task; install with
# `go install github.com/go-task/task/v3/cmd/task@latest`.
#
# THIS CHECK RUNS BEFORE THE `skip` VERDICT IS HONOURED, deliberately. It used to sit
# after it, so the mutex ref and an empty push exited 0 on a machine with no toolchain —
# and the written invariant ("no task -> refusal, never green") was false as written. The
# ONE exception is the `delete` class handled above, by name and in the docs; a `skip` is
# not it. "Nothing to gate" decided by a rule the machine could not run is still an
# unexamined push, and footnotes are exactly how "the gate did not run" became
# indistinguishable from "the gate passed" before.
if ! command -v task >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	# ${HOME:-} y no $HOME: bajo `set -u` un runner sin HOME —seis de nueve, medido— mata
	# el hook aqui mismo, y el mensaje que sale no habla de HOME sino de una linea suelta.
	if [ -x "${HOME:-/nonexistent-home}/go/bin/task" ]; then
		PATH="${HOME:-/nonexistent-home}/go/bin:$PATH"
	else
		echo "pre-push: 'task' not found — the local gate CANNOT run, refusing the push." >&2
		echo "pre-push: install go-task (go install github.com/go-task/task/v3/cmd/task@latest)" >&2
		echo "pre-push: or bypass EXPLICITLY with: git push --no-verify" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
fi

# ⛔ GUARDA DEL LECTOR DE CLAIMS, Y VA AQUI: ANTES DE HONRAR EL `skip`.
#
# Un claim se clasifica `skip` —«signalling, never content»— y eso es correcto para su CONTENIDO:
# no hay nada que lintar. Pero de ahi se seguia algo que nadie decidio: que MOVER un claim no pasa
# por ningun control. `scripts/claim-lector.sh` publica la señal de que alguien lo esta leyendo, y
# mientras la comprobacion viviera solo en ese guion era una cortesia: quien no lo corriera, movia.
#
# Esta guarda solo mira ACTUALIZACIONES de un claim existente. Un alta (oid remoto todo ceros) no
# puede pisar a nadie, y un borrado tiene su propia excepcion con nombre mas arriba. El coste, por
# tanto, lo paga unicamente la operacion que puede hacer daño.
#
# FALLA CERRADO: si no puedo preguntarle al remoto si hay lector, RECHAZO. «No he podido mirar» no
# es «no habia nadie», que es la confusion que este repositorio lleva todo el dia corrigiendo.
#
# ⚠ Y LO QUE ESTA GUARDA NO ES, dicho aqui para que nadie lo lea como mas de lo que da: NO es
#   atomica. Entre este `ls-remote` y el push cabe que alguien tome la señal, y entonces el claim
#   se mueve igual. Cerrar esa ventana desde un hook de cliente no se puede —la gramatica de
#   `--force-with-lease` compara SIEMPRE el ref que empuja consigo mismo—. Lo que hay DESPUES no
#   es otra guarda sino DETECCION: la señal pincha el SHA leido, asi que `claim-lector.sh libre`
#   contesta «MOVIDO BAJO LECTOR» comparandola con el valor de ahora. Nombra el movimiento
#   cuando ya ocurrio; no lo impide.
#
#   ⚠ Y LA DETECCION TAMPOCO CIERRA LA VENTANA — decirlo asi era vender de mas, y un lector lo
#   midio: `libre` hace DOS `ls-remote` (señal y claim) y entre ellos cabe que aparezca una
#   señal. Eso ya no da una respuesta falsa —se relee y se contesta 2, «no he podido mirar»—
#   pero 2 no es «cerrado»: es fallar cerrado. La guarda ESTRECHA la ventana de minutos a
#   milisegundos y la deteccion la CUBRE despues, nombrando el movimiento cuando ya ocurrio.
#   Nada de esto es exclusion mutua, y quien lo lea tiene que saberlo antes de confiarle algo.
_cl_ceros() { case "$1" in *[!0]*) return 1 ;; *) return 0 ;; esac; }
_cl_rechazo=0
while read -r _cl_lref _cl_loid _cl_rref _cl_roid; do
	case "$_cl_rref" in
	refs/integration-claims/*.lector) continue ;;
	refs/integration-claims/?*) ;;
	*) continue ;;
	esac
	_cl_ceros "$_cl_roid" && continue   # alta: no hay nada bajo lo que meterse
	_cl_ceros "$_cl_loid" && continue   # borrado: su excepcion esta arriba, con nombre
	_cl_sig="refs/integration-claims/${_cl_rref#refs/integration-claims/}.lector"
	if ! _cl_out="$(git ls-remote "${OLIVARES_PUSH_REMOTE_NAME:-origin}" "$_cl_sig" 2>/dev/null)"; then
		echo "pre-push: NO HE PODIDO MIRAR si '$_cl_rref' tiene lector — rechazo el push." >&2
		_cl_rechazo=1
		continue
	fi
	[ -n "$_cl_out" ] || continue
	echo "pre-push: '$_cl_rref' TIENE LECTOR y este push lo mueve ($_cl_roid -> $_cl_loid)." >&2
	echo "pre-push:   $(printf '%s' "$_cl_out" | awk 'NR==1{print $1}')  $_cl_sig" >&2
	echo "pre-push:   quien lo lee, con: bash scripts/claim-lector.sh libre ${_cl_rref#refs/integration-claims/}" >&2
	echo "pre-push:   publica un claim NUEVO, o espera a que suelte, o pasa con --no-verify y declaralo." >&2
	_cl_rechazo=1
done <"$push_refs_file"
if [ "$_cl_rechazo" != "0" ]; then
