# Identity, and it runs here for the same reason disk-headroom does: it is about the state the rest
# of this file assumes, not about the diff. A private address in a commit is not undone by a later
# fix — the commit is already on the server.
# ⛔ ITS ENVIRONMENT IS SCRUBBED FIRST, and this is not tidiness. The gate reads OLIVARES_ROOT to
# know WHICH repository to judge, and OLIVARES_COMMIT_EMAIL to read the rule it enforces. Both are
# legitimate for the battery, which drives the gate directly; NEITHER has any business surviving
# into a real push. The the model contrast of 2026-08-12 walked in through both: with the
# predicate pointed at the private address the gate answered CLEAN over a commit carrying it, and
# with the root pointed at another clone it judged a repository nobody was pushing. Two ways to
# make the identity gate agree with whatever the caller wanted.
#
# It is not a subshell for style: the unsets must not leak into the lints that follow either, so
# the leg runs inside one and the environment is restored by the parenthesis closing.
#
# Carried over from hub/identity-gate-failclosed, whose other half is superseded -- main already
# fixes the published-history range (with TRUNK, a further refinement over --remotes) and already
# answers UNVERIFIED where that branch found CLEAN. This hardening is the part that had no
# equivalent, so it lands on its own rather than riding a 44-hour-stale merge of three rewritten
# security gates.
(
	# ⛔ FOUR, not two. The scrub landed covering OLIVARES_ROOT and OLIVARES_COMMIT_EMAIL, which are
	# the two that decide WHICH repository and WHICH address. The gate's own header names FOUR
	# values as overridable and singles out the CUTOFF as the one the Codex sol-max contrast
	# actually demonstrated (report S-07): an ambient cutoff in the future makes a push carrying a
	# private address print CLEAN. Measured here 2026-08-15 against main, with a real commit
	# authored with the private consumer-mail address this gate forbids (the literal is
	# deliberately NOT written here: it ships in .githooks/, so a comment naming it puts it
	# in the public tree — measured on 2026-08-29 by the R1 dry-run export) and a real
	# push-refs file:
	#
	#   OLIVARES_IDENTITY_CUTOFF='2027-01-01 00:00'  ->  CLEAN, 0 commit(s) examined
	#
	# The compensating control does work -- the verdict printed the moved cutoff and the zero, so
	# the weakened run does not read like a strong one. But a scrub written precisely so the push
	# path cannot inherit these knobs, that then inherits half of them, is a scrub whose reader
	# reasonably believes the push path is sealed. Both remaining knobs default to what this hook
	# wants anyway (cutoff 2026-08-10, trunk origin/main), so unsetting them removes a bypass and
	# changes no legitimate verdict.
	#
	# This does NOT refuse the overrides in the gate itself, and must not: its battery needs them
	# to build hermetic cases, which is the reason main gives for keeping them and it is right.
	unset OLIVARES_ROOT OLIVARES_COMMIT_EMAIL OLIVARES_IDENTITY_CUTOFF OLIVARES_IDENTITY_TRUNK
	task lint:commit-identity
)
